  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Friday)\nIssued at 146 AM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nWhile next week still looks to be dominated by a continued hot \nand dry pattern, especially on Tuesday and most of Wednesday, a \npathway for more changeable conditions, including at least low \nchances for precipitation, is coming into better view into the \nlate week. \n\nThis comes as a strong upper system rounds the periphery of the \nupper ridge into portions of the Upper Midwest/Great Lakes. A weak\ntrailing front looks to slide southeastward across the Plains \nduring this time. The greatest uncertainty remains 1.) how strong \nthe resultant front is (i.e., able to help foster precipitative \nchance?), and 2.) how far south the front makes it before \ndissipating. For now, we maintain at least low rain/storm chances \nfrom Wednesday night into Thursday across the northern half of\nOklahoma. Not seeing much potential for a significant cool down, \nthough daytime highs may run a few degrees below those early in \nthe week.\n\nEven greater uncertainty exists beyond the end of the period (12th \nonward), though most ensemble solutions show the upper ridge \nremaining the dominant regional synoptic feature during this time.\n\nUngar",
